title: Claude Stop-hook c2c delivery must not surface a message payload as Stop hook error
updated_at: "2026-07-12T15:40:02Z"
---
Problem

A real Claude Code session ran the installed c2c stop-delivery hook and rendered the inbound c2c XML envelope plus system reminder under `Stop hook error:`. The message itself was delivered, but presenting valid peer mail as a hook failure is misleading and makes normal stop-hook activity look broken.

Observed shape

Claude reported `Ran 6 stop hooks`, then the c2c payload (`<c2c event="message" ...>...</c2c>` plus the untrusted-peer reminder) directly after `Stop hook error:`. Current c2c tests intentionally assert that the stop hook emits full messages, so determine whether stdout is being interpreted as an invalid Claude Stop-hook protocol response, an expected but wrong transport convention, or a genuine nonzero/stderr failure.

Relevant code

Trace `C2c_hook_cmd.hook_stop_cmd` / `hook_stop`, generated `claude_stop_hook_script`, and the installed `~/.claude-p/hooks/c2c-stop-deliver.sh`. Preserve the message-bus safety invariant: peer content stays model-visible data and never becomes an approval/action.

Acceptance criteria

1. Reproduce against the installed Claude Code version with a controlled c2c message and capture exit code, stdout, stderr, and Claude-rendered result.
2. Verify the current Claude Stop-hook output contract from authoritative docs/runtime behavior.
3. If c2c is violating the contract, change the delivery encoding/channel so valid inbound mail is surfaced without a `Stop hook error`, while maintaining turn-boundary delivery and no-message silent exit behavior.
4. If raw emitted payload is required by Claude, document why the UI labels it as an error and identify a supported alternative or explicitly record that it is unavoidable.
5. Add regression coverage for the actual hook protocol (including exit status and output shape) and dogfood alongside the other installed stop hooks.

Blockers

Avoid swallowing or redirecting peer messages to a channel Claude does not make model-visible; delivery correctness is more important than cosmetic suppression.